  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining Key Fields**\n\nI\'m identifying the key data points to extract: rating (checking for \"score\" usage), release year (\"year\"), episode count (\"episodes\"), studio, status, and genres. My focus is on ensuring these are cleanly retrieved and displayed. Next step is determining the best methods of retrieval.\n\n\n**Expanding Data Acquisition**\n\nI\'m now focusing on image fields (poster, banner), synopsis, and the crucial format and season details. I\'ll make sure to add `format` and `season` as optional or explicit fields in `src/types.ts`. I need to ensure the system gracefully handles missing data from providers, filling gaps where possible and displaying \"Unknown\" only as a last resort. I\'m prioritizing data integrity and preventing the display of incomplete or zero-value metadata. I\'m focusing on the logic needed to query multiple sources and determine the appropriate behavior for incomplete data, before displaying the results.\n\n\n**Refining Data Retrieval**\n\nI\'ve got a robust plan: if key data, such as studio, banner, or synopsis, is missing, or if the score is zero, I\'ll initiate a parallel or fallback fetch to Jikan, using the MAL ID, and merge the results. The fetch flow will start with AniList, map the media, and check for missing fields. Then, if needed, Jikan\'s `/anime/${id}/full` endpoint will supply the missing data, which can then be combined with the initial AniList fetch. I need to make sure to account for ID differences between systems.\n\n\n**Confirming Key Identifiers**\n\nI\'ve confirmed how the application represents IDs. The critical ID everywhere is `mal_id`. It pulls from `media.idMal` when available and falls back to `media.id`. I\'m now certain I can reliably use this ID to query Jikan. Moreover, I\'m developing a merge function for integrating data from both providers when they\'re both available, which should make the process much more robust and complete.\n\n\n**Refining Data Integration**\n\nI\'m now implementing the merge function. I\'m prioritizing data from `target` (AniList) and then falling back to `source` (Jikan). The merging logic now includes checks for empty or default values in the `target`, using data from `source` if the AniList source has those default values. Specifically, I\'m checking genres, synopsis, score, popularity, type, episodes, status, year, studio, image, and bannerImage.\n\n\n**Finalizing Data Merging**\n\nI\'ve just finished the final enhancements to the merge function. It prioritizes `target` (AniList) data, falling back to `source` (Jikan) for missing or default values. It now includes checks for `bannerImage`, `trailer`, `format`, `season`, `characters`, and `relations`. The system defaults to \"Unknown\" for `season` only if both providers are empty. This ensures that the application displays the most complete and accurate metadata available online, using the logic: \"If one provider is missing a field, automatically retrieve it from the other provider before displaying placeholders. Only display \'Unknown\' if both providers fail. Never display 0.0 ratings or empty metadata when valid data exists online.\" I\'m also confirming the approach for the combined parallel and fallback fetch. I\'ll now double-check the detail fetch implementation for both providers to ensure the highest data quality.\n\n\n**Implementing Data Caching**\n\nI\'m now implementing `getOnlineAnimeDetails`. The flow is: first check the cache, then try AniList (with `idMal` then `id`), then try Jikan (with `id`). If both succeed, merge; otherwise, use the successful one. I\'ve considered rate limiting: if AniList succeeds but needs data, I\'ll attempt a Jikan fetch within a `try...catch` to avoid crashes. Conversely, if Jikan succeeds, I\'ll attempt to supplement from AniList within a similar `try...catch`. I\'m also adding `format` and `season` to the `Anime` interface.\n\n\n**Adding GraphQL Integration**\n\nI\'m now integrating the `format` and `season` fields into the AniList GraphQL query and the `types.ts` file. I\'ve confirmed that the AniList media type correctly supports both `format` and `season`. The GraphQL query will be updated to fetch these fields. I need to make sure the mapping handles the conversion of `MediaFormat` and `MediaSeason` values, and I\'ll verify the new fields are correctly integrated.\n\n\n"
