    thinking: "**Analyzing Recommendation Limits**\n\nI\'ve been examining the `ContentRecommender` in `src/utils/recommender.ts`, and how it currently leverages TF-IDF, genres, studio, type, and synopsis for recommendations. My immediate focus is on adapting it to requirement 3. Specifically, increasing the display limit from 5-10 recommendations to 30-50. This seems like a good starting point.\n\n\n**Combining Recommendation Sources**\n\nI\'m now focused on how recommendations are fetched and combined server-side, specifically within the `/api/recommend/:id` route in `server.ts`. It seems we currently: load anime details, fetch AniList recommendations, build a `ContentRecommender`, and run it. The goal is to build a hybrid algorithm. This will combine AniList recommendations with genres, themes, studio, TF-IDF, and cosine similarity. I\'ll sort by overall relevance and quality to reach the high-quality targets.\n\n\n**Expanding Recommendation Scope**\n\nI\'m now thinking about recommendation candidates, which brings up the `globalAnimePool` limitation. This pool of preloaded items won\'t provide a diverse enough candidate pool for a wider 30-50 result set. Therefore, I\'m now looking at how to build a robust candidate pool. My current approach involves fetching live recommendations from AniList and Jikan for the anime ID, let\'s say 20-30 results. Furthermore, I\'ll leverage AniList and Jikan to search for other shows with similar genres.\n\n\n**Refining Candidate Sourcing**\n\nI\'m now iterating on candidate sourcing. I\'ll fetch popular shows sharing genres with the source anime (up to 50). Moreover, I\'ll leverage AniList or Jikan for studio-based recommendations. These additions will augment the `globalAnimePool` significantly. This aggregate candidate pool (80-100 shows) becomes the foundation for hybrid scoring. I\'m excited about the dynamic potential.\n\n\n**Optimizing Recommendation Retrieval**\n\nI\'ve made a breakthrough. I realized I can adjust the `perPage` limit in the AniList GraphQL query within `getOnlineRecommendations` to fetch 40 or 50 recommendations directly. This significantly boosts the initial candidate pool\'s size, improving efficiency. Furthermore, I\'ll leverage Jikan for supplementary recommendations. I can also query AniList for highly popular shows within the source anime\'s genres to further enrich the pool, and I\'ll implement a helper function for this purpose.\n\n\n**Expanding Candidate Pool**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a new function, `getRecommendationCandidates`. My goal is to dynamically build a larger and more diverse pool. First, I\'ll fetch AniList recommendations directly, up to 40 items. Then, I\'m considering fetching anime from the same studio, although the details on how best to query the data are still being worked out. I\'m excited by this approach.\n\n\n**Refining Candidate Filtering**\n\nI\'ve been working on a new approach for filtering candidates. I\'m focusing on incorporating a global pool of anime, to enrich the results. I\'m currently using an if statement that takes the first two genres and returns popular anime with those genres. It looks like it is working great to improve the initial recommendations.\n\n\n**Designing Scoring Formula**\n\nI\'m now designing a scoring formula for each candidate anime. I\'ll calculate TF-IDF similarity, genre similarity using the Jaccard index, and theme similarity (overlapping themes). I plan to include a studio similarity factor. All will be normalized. This will allow for more informed recommendations.\n\n\n\n**Calculating Theme Similarity**\n\nI\'m now refining the scoring formula by incorporating theme similarity. I\'m extracting themes from synopses and genres, then calculating overlaps between the source and candidate anime. I\'m using a predefined theme list for this. I\'ve developed code to calculate the overlap and normalize the score. I\'m also including a studio similarity factor to provide a boost if the studios match.\n\n\n"
