  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining Merge Logic**\n\nI\'ve got the mergeAnimeMetadata logic clear in my mind now. I\'m focusing on implementation, specifically how to best write the function signature. I\'m defining a utility helper within `src/utils/liveAnimeProvider.ts` to keep things tidy and reusable. I think this will greatly improve modularity.\n\n\n**Refining Merge Implementation**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the mergeAnimeMetadata implementation. The logic to handle edge cases and prioritize certain values over others is forming quite nicely. I\'m focusing on the various fields of anime data, like titles and synopsis, defining clear rules for which source wins, handling undefined or empty values elegantly. I think I\'m making a good approach towards ensuring that the merge function produces a complete and accurate output.\n\n\n**Determining Merge Triggers**\n\nI\'m now determining *when* the metadata merge should occur. It seems logical to perform it after fetching online details using `getOnlineAnimeDetails(id)`. Specifically, I\'m thinking about fetching from AniList first, based on MAL ID, with a fallback to AniList ID. Then, if AniList\'s data is incomplete (lacking studio information, for instance), we can initiate the merge process.\n\n\n**Defining Fetch Priorities**\n\nI\'ve got the fetch priorities and fallback mechanisms firmly established. If AniList provides incomplete data (like a missing score, banner, or synopsis), I\'ll attempt a Jikan fetch and merge the results. If AniList fails entirely, Jikan becomes the primary source. If Jikan is primary, I\'ll attempt an AniList search to fill in missing fields, such as the banner image, from AniList. The data flow is now fully bidirectional. I\'m focusing on crafting the implementation of `getOnlineAnimeDetails`.\n\n\n**Implementing Caching Strategy**\n\nI\'m now implementing the caching strategy for `getOnlineAnimeDetails`. I\'m going to leverage an in-memory cache to reduce redundant API calls and optimize performance. I\'m focusing on defining cache keys and setting expiration times to strike the right balance between freshness and efficiency. I\'m storing the responses using a simple in memory cache with an expiry date, and this looks to perform well.\n\nI\'ve also structured the logic to prioritize AniList, then Jikan, with clear fallbacks and merge points.\n\n\n**Analyzing GraphQL Responses**\n\nI\'m now analyzing the structure of GraphQL responses, specifically focusing on how to extract and map data from the \"edges\" and \"node\" fields for related anime, characters, and recommendations. I\'m focusing on parsing character information, mapping voice actors, and extracting recommendations, with an eye towards efficient data extraction. I\'m taking the fields from the GraphQL response and mapping them to our internal data structures.\n\n\n**Refining GraphQL Fetch Logic**\n\nI\'m now refining the GraphQL fetch logic to include error handling and fallbacks. I\'m focusing on ensuring data retrieval by attempting a search based on `idMal` first. If that fails, I\'ll fall back to searching directly by `id`. I\'m using `fetchWithRetry` for reliability. I have a clear plan for parsing the GraphQL responses and handling various scenarios where specific data might be missing, or the initial API requests are unsuccessful.\n\n\n**Evaluating Fetch Conditions**\n\nI\'m now evaluating the conditions for triggering a Jikan fetch. I will fetch Jikan details if AniList data is missing key metadata, or if the initial AniList request fails. I\'m focusing on conditions where data is absent or incomplete to determine when I need the secondary Jikan data.\n\n\n**Implementing Jikan Fallback**\n\nI\'m now implementing the Jikan fallback and bi-directional merge logic. I\'m handling the scenarios where only Jikan data is available, initiating a search on AniList by title to fetch banner images and recommendations, if a MAL id match is found, and merging the results. I\'m focusing on ensuring d